Exactly. That's why I specified "to a person who knows neither".

Aside from the apostrophe/h and more frequent sh/'c' sound, what's the real
difference? They have the same word shapes, basically the same sounds. Is
there any distinctive intonation pattern in either language?

stevo

On Mon, Feb 8, 2016 at 12:44 PM, 'John E Clifford' via lojban <
lojban@googlegroups.com> wrote:

> It seems the question is about the general sound, not about individual
> sentences, which are obviously different, given the relex and all.
>
>
>
> On Monday, February 8, 2016 12:00 PM, Gleki Arxokuna <
> gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>
>
>
> 2016-02-08 19:43 GMT+03:00 MorphemeAddict <lytlesw@gmail.com>:
>
> It seems to me that, except for a few minor differences like long vowels
> and syllable-initial /h/, Loglan and Lojban should sound almost identical
> to a person who knows neither. Is this true, or is there a bigger
> difference I'm missing?
>
>
> They sound very different.
>
> Try the tool http://mw.lojban.org/extensions/ilmentufa/ircbot/naxle.html
>
> and after typing something in Lojban press "Loglan < Lojban"  to try
> automatic conversion to Loglan. although the conversion is not perfect it
> can show you the difference.
>
> E.g. {mi prami do} => {mi cluva tu}.
>
>
> stevo
